Opt-in pgbench workload
======================

    make -C src/test/modules/test_page_store check \
      PROVE_TESTS='bench/pgbench.pl'

This starts a writer, two following readers, page/redo storage, and an
independent WAL inbox.  All pgbench heaps and B-tree indexes have no local
main/VM/FSM files on the three computes.  Other relations, catalogs, SLRUs,
and WAL staging remain local.  By default, nodes are stopped after the run;
the interactive mode below delays that cleanup.  Never point this fixture
at existing data.

The writer runs rate-limited simple-update with two clients, while each
reader runs select-only with two clients.  The defaults are scale 1,
5 seconds and 100 writer transactions/second.  Optional environment:

    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_SECONDS=30        # 3..60
    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_RATE=200          # 1..200, aggregate writer rate
    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_SCALE=1           # 1..4
    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_MODE=remote       # remote or local
    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_REVISION=...      # include the tested source revision
    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_HOLD_SECONDS=0    # 0..3600, manual inspection after checks

Use -O2 without assertions for measurements.  Assertions/debug builds are
useful only for workload correctness.  Each run gets a fresh cluster; save
tmp_check/log/pgbench-*.json and server logs before the next make check.
JSON includes the exact configure flags, commands, raw pgbench output,
history capacity and replay/overlay counters.  Do not infer concurrency
from throughput alone: the final data/TID comparisons and counters check
that writes happened and both following readers used remote storage.
Each client must report positive transaction counts and zero failures,
even if pgbench exits with status zero.  The writer's processed count must
equal the number of committed pgbench_history rows.

fsync is on, and writer flushes wait for the independent inbox.  The page
history is volatile, append-only, bounded, and linearly searched; it is not
a performance-oriented provider.  Report measurements as prototype costs,
not as the expected performance of compute/storage separation.  History
headroom is checked; no capacity failure may be counted as a valid sample.

Local-files mode leaves the same five nodes, history collection and WAL
durability path running, but uses ordinary md files on writer and readers.
This is a controlled reference for remote access overhead, not a benchmark
of a conventional three-node PostgreSQL cluster.  No crash/failover is
injected during measurement; separate TAP tests cover those transitions.

Interactive demo
----------------

To inspect the same five-node setup after the workload and its checks:

    PAGE_STORE_BENCH_HOLD_SECONDS=1800 \
      make -C src/test/modules/test_page_store check \
      PROVE_TESTS='bench/pgbench.pl' PROVE_FLAGS='-v'

Wait for tmp_check/log/pgbench-demo.json in this module.  It is published
only after the correctness checks pass, and lists the connstr, pgdata and
logfile for each node.  In another terminal, from the checkout root:

    demo=src/test/modules/test_page_store/tmp_check/log/pgbench-demo.json
    src/bin/psql/psql \
      "$(jq -r '.nodes.bench_r1.connstr' "$demo")"

Alternatively, use psql from your build's install prefix.  bench_writer
accepts writes; bench_r1 and bench_r2 are following readers.  The other
two nodes are storage services, not additional computes.  Keep changes
small: history and overlay are bounded, and only the existing seven
pgbench relations are selected for remote I/O.

To stop early, run:

    touch "$(jq -r '.resume_file' "$demo")"

Otherwise, wait for the requested hold interval.  The normal harness
cleanup stops all five nodes.  Ctrl-C also triggers cleanup but reports
an interrupted test, not a successful one.  The JSON file can remain
after shutdown; its presence alone does not prove the nodes are still
running.  Do not try to restart the old
writer PGDATA after this session: its exact-image overlay is volatile.
Start a new demo instead.  This phase is not part of the measured workload;
manual changes made here are not included in the preceding test assertions.
